{"author":{"id":"038d65070aa747b6bc9d50939fa697ec871a40ed259db54429b255bbef2581d6"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792794370,"nonce":"MnalNyhuOBxA6lcsZwLnMPUXy0c=","title":"Plan RayDE accounts and hosted Jam tiers","message":"Requested scope: plan RayDE account management and product tiers for hosted Jam collaboration, private collaborator audio/video and optional user-selected public streaming. This is a proposed product/implementation plan, not implemented functionality or published pricing. Existing local editor features remain account-free in the proposal.\n\nProduct boundaries\n- Local editing, Rayzor/Haxe language support, Git, build/run, extensions, previews and local history remain available without a RayDE account, including offline. Local OBS control and recording are not subscription gates.\n- An account enables hosted Jam rooms, cloud assets/checkpoints, team membership, optional preferences sync and usage/billing. Signing in does not upload projects, LSP caches or credentials. Content sharing and preferences sync are explicit choices.\n- A Jam has one explicit billing owner: a personal space or a team. Invited guests do not need subscriptions; they count toward the room's participant limit and their usage is charged to that owner. No automatic billing-owner switch when the host disconnects.\n- Public Twitch/YouTube/etc accounts are optional linked identities, separate from RayDE sign-in and team permissions. Membership of a private call does not grant permission to rebroadcast someone.\n\nIdentity and desktop experience\n- Stable internal user IDs, separate provider issuer/subject identities, private verified emails, display names, handles and avatars. Never automatically merge accounts merely because emails match.\n- Launch sign-in with GitHub and email through a managed identity provider behind an OIDC adapter. Select the identity vendor after testing browser redirects, recovery, account export/deletion and pricing. Add passkeys/recovery/MFA where supported, with strong authentication for sensitive ownership/billing actions.\n- Native sign-in uses the system browser with authorization code + PKCE and state; device-code fallback only for providers that support it. Never embed an OAuth client secret in RayDE.\n- App-level AccountManager/CredentialStore coalesces refresh and shares session state across project windows. Each window independently identifies its workspace/team context. OS credential storage owns refresh/provider credentials; ordinary settings/session files contain only non-secret identifiers/preferences. No plaintext token fallback.\n- Accounts settings provides Profile, Connected Accounts, Devices and Sessions, Teams, Plan and Usage, Billing, Export/Delete. Sign-out/revocation releases cloud connections and media while local editing continues. Account/cloud tasks stay off the UI thread and do not add idle polling to a signed-out window.\n- Extensions receive approved profile fields and brokered provider operations, never RayDE bearer tokens. Approved native libraries remain trusted OS code; existing host process isolation is not an OS security sandbox.\n\nAuthorization model\n- Separate user identity, organization membership, billing ownership, Jam role and effective feature/limit grants.\n- Team roles: owner, admin, billing manager, member and guest. Room roles: host/moderator, editor and spectator. Editing, publishing microphone/camera/screen, rebroadcasting and running code/terminal commands are distinct grants.\n- Every room request and media create/subscribe/close operation checks current server authority. Invite tokens are expiring, scoped, revocable and limited-use. Revocation closes existing media subscriptions/tracks as needed, not just hiding them in discovery. Test isolation across personal spaces, teams and parallel project windows.\n\nProvisional tier matrix (USD/month; assumptions to validate with beta measurements)\n- Local: $0, no account, full local IDE; no hosted allocation.\n- Community: $0 account, 4 participants/room including host, 1 simultaneous hosted Jam, 10 GB/month hosted media egress, 1 GB cloud storage, 7-day cloud checkpoint retention.\n- Jam Plus: proposed $12/month, 8 participants/room, 2 simultaneous hosted Jams, 60 GB/month media, 10 GB cloud storage, 30-day cloud checkpoint retention.\n- Teams: proposed $10/paid member/month, minimum 3 paid members; 16 participants/room; simultaneous Jams up to paid member count; 60 GB media and 10 GB storage per paid member pooled across the team; 90-day cloud checkpoint retention; team permissions, consolidated billing and administrative history. Paid members can create/manage team workspaces and rooms; room-scoped guests can collaborate free. Changes to paid seats require explicit billing authority and a charge preview.\n- All hosted tiers include private code collaboration, voice/camera/screen sharing within allowances, and choice of streaming provider. No watermark or resolution paywall; adapt media quality to devices and bandwidth. Public platform distribution is outside RayDE's media meter; guest media delivered into OBS counts as a subscription to those tracks.\n- Launch Community and Jam Plus first. Prepare Teams in the schema but release after invitations, billing ownership and usage enforcement are proven. Consider a prepaid time-limited Jam Pass for occasional game-jam weekends later; price it from measured usage. Do not promise unlimited media, cloud recording, free lifetime cloud hosting or unmeasured SLAs.\n\nPricing and usage rules\n- Media is a pooled egress allowance, with current and estimated consumption visible. Show an indicative remaining-call estimate with bitrate/participant assumptions; never advertise an invariant number of hours for a GB allowance.\n- Cloudflare's current raw SFU/TURN marginal price is $0.05/GB; the 1,000 GB shared provider allowance belongs to our Cloudflare account, not each customer. Model unit costs above that allowance: 10 GB = $0.50 and 60 GB = $3 in media egress alone, before compute, assets, auth, payment fees, support and abuse. Final prices need a unit-economics review.\n- Prototype trusted per-room egress attribution, reporting delay, quota reservations and reconciliation before promising exact per-GB customer billing. Endpoint statistics are estimates, not authoritative invoices. Do not launch postpaid usage charging until attribution is validated. The provider's WebSocket-adapter bytesProcessed is expressly not an authoritative billed total.\n- Reserve bounded usage budgets per active room against one serialized billing-owner ledger, renew/reconcile idempotently, and aggregate accounting away from editor messages. Do not write billing records per keystroke or packet.\n- Default extra spending is disabled. Notify at 80/95 percent, offer quality reduction/audio-only while allowance remains, or explicit prepaid top-ups. At exhaustion block new hosted media and stop active media after a visible, bounded grace/countdown; preserve code collaboration within its own limits and all local work. Never silently charge a guest.\n- Free allocations attach to verified eligible owners, not every disposable workspace; apply account-creation/hosting rate limits, room/connection limits and operator-wide cost controls.\n\nCloudflare and billing architecture\n- Worker API: native/browser auth handoff, sessions, invites, provider OAuth callbacks, entitlement checks and scoped URLs.\n- D1: users, identity mappings, personal/team spaces, memberships, devices, subscription projections and searchable account metadata.\n- JamRoom Durable Object + SQLite: current room authority, connections, document update/checkpoint references, temporary presence and media resource ownership.\n- UsageAccount Durable Object + SQLite: authoritative quota reservations and idempotent usage ledger per personal/team billing owner; D1 receives query/reporting projections.\n- R2: authorized assets, checkpoints and explicit exports; SFU/TURN: collaborator media; Queues: verified billing-event processing, reconciliation and deferred cleanup. Keep streaming adapters behind a provider interface.\n- Stripe hosted Checkout + Customer Portal is the initial proposed payment integration. Verify webhook signatures using raw bodies, deduplicate event IDs, tolerate out-of-order events, retrieve current provider state when reconciling, and keep RayDE-owned versioned entitlements. The checkout return URL and desktop plan labels never grant access. Hosted services enforce feature flags and numerical limits independently of client UI.\n- Upgrades become effective after confirmed billing state; cancellation normally applies at period end; failed payments receive a bounded visible grace policy. Downgrades preserve local files and offer a defined cloud export/over-quota grace period. Define ownership transfer, cloud retention and deletion behavior before public launch.\n\nRollout and acceptance\n1. Identity, secure credential storage and app-level Account settings; preserve signed-out/offline startup and baseline idle behavior.\n2. Personal/team ownership, scoped invitations and server-enforced Jam/media roles in a capped pilot.\n3. Shadow usage accounting and operational budget controls, validated against provider reporting; no variable customer charges.\n4. Versioned entitlements, payment sandbox, signed webhook processing, portal and Community/Plus launch with validated allowances/prices.\n5. Teams, pooled allowances, administrative controls, optional preferences sync and a possible prepaid Jam Pass.\nAcceptance includes account-link takeover/recovery, wrong-team access, expired invites, token refresh concurrency across windows, revoked device/media cleanup, duplicate/out-of-order billing events, quota races across rooms, reconnect/crash recovery, downgrade/export behavior and no authentication work in a signed-out idle editor. Free/paid source sharing remains explicit.\n\nSources reviewed 2026-10-23\nhttps://www.rfc-editor.org/info/rfc8252/\nhttps://docs.github.com/en/apps/oauth-apps/building-oauth-apps/authorizing-oauth-apps\nhttps://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/platform/storage-options/\nhttps://developers.cloudflare.com/realtime/sfu/platform/pricing/\nhttps://developers.cloudflare.com/realtime/sfu/api/\nhttps://developers.cloudflare.com/realtime/sfu/concepts/sessions-tracks/\nhttps://docs.stripe.com/payments/checkout\nhttps://docs.stripe.com/customer-management\nhttps://docs.stripe.com/webhooks","files":null}]}